.0283966064453125, -0.01143646240234375, -0.021514892578125, -0.008941650390625, 0.00600433349609375, 0.0031223297119140625, 0.0035152435302734375, -0.0020294189453125, -0.0229644775390625, 0.0111541748046875, 0.0253143310546875, 0.029876708984375, 0.01314544677734375, -0.028289794921875, 0.0175933837890625, -0.01351165771484375, 0.0019483566284179688, -0.01076507568359375, 0.002536773681640625, -0.006603240966796875, -0.01558685302734375, 0.004940032958984375, 0.006923675537109375, 0.00943756103515625, 0.0172576904296875, 0.0300445556640625, 0.022552490234375, -0.005863189697265625, -0.0209503173828125, 6.222724914550781e-05, 0.0119171142578125, -0.008941650390625, 0.01088714599609375, -0.0022602081298828125, -0.013824462890625, 0.005664825439453125, -0.01345062255859375, -0.0192718505859375, -0.0026340484619140625, -0.0157470703125, 0.012115478515625, -0.048583984375, -0.003448486328125, -0.0102081298828125, -0.0297393798828125, 0.002716064453125, -0.0311431884765625, 0.005985260009765625, -0.01081085205078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nIntroduction\n\nMemory-based self-improving agents are learning systems that incrementally enhance their performance by explicitly storing and retrieving structured records of prior experiences. In this work, we address a critical gap in the literature: the fragility of such agents\u2014their sensitivity to stochasticity and task ordering\u2014which has been underexplored despite the increasing deployment of memory-augmented agents in high-stakes domains. Our central research question is: *How and why do memory-based self-improving agents exhibit fragility in sequential decision-making tasks, and what are the underlying causes?* Here, we define *fragility* as the pronounced variability in agent performance due to small changes in task order or environmental stochasticity, and *memory underspecification* as the lack of explicit constraints or standards for what information is stored, how it is structured, and how it is retrieved\u2014leading to inconsistent or incomplete memory content. These concepts are related to the broader literature on catastrophic forgetting [5], curriculum learning [6], and memory system design [8], but have not been systematically examined in the context of explicit, structured memory agents.\n\n**Operational Definition and Taxonomy**\n\nWe define a *memory-based self-improving agent* as an agent that (1) maintains a persistent, structured, and queryable memory of prior experiences (in textual or vectorized form), (2) uses this memory to inform future decisions, and (3) updates its memory online as new tasks are encountered. This excludes agents with only implicit memory (e.g., standard RNNs or transformers without explicit memory modules) and those using non-persistent or unstructured caches. Table 1 summarizes included and excluded agent types.\n\n| Agent Type                                 | Explicit Structured Memory | Online Update | Included? | Rationale for Inclusion/Exclusion |\n|--------------------------------------------|---------------------------|---------------|-----------|-----------------------------------|\n| Retrieval-augmented LM agents [3]          | Yes                       | Yes           | Yes       | Prevalent, explicit memory, online update |\n| Memory-augmented RL agents [4]             | Yes                       | Yes           | Yes       | Prevalent, explicit memory, online update |\n| Differentiable Neural Computers            | Yes                       | Yes           | No        | Excluded due to architectural complexity and limited adoption in practical domains; focus is on agents with discrete, interpretable memory modules |\n| Standard RNN/Transformer (no ext. memory)  | No                        | N/A           | No        | Implicit memory only              |\n| Agents with unstructured replay buffers    | No                        | N/A           | No        | Memory is unstructured and non-queryable |\n\nOur focus is on retrieval-augmented language model (LM) agents and memory-augmented RL agents, as these are prevalent in recent literature and widely used in domains where unpredictable failures can have significant consequences (e.g., automated customer support [1], adaptive tutoring [2], robotics [3,4]). We do not address agents with implicit or non-persistent memory, nor do we attempt a comprehensive taxonomy of all possible architectures. The selection of these two architectures is motivated by their interpretability, prevalence, and the feasibility of systematic analysis. While our findings may not generalize to all memory-based agents (e.g., differentiable memory architectures), we believe the observed fragility mechanisms are relevant to a broad class of agents with explicit, structured, and persistent memory.\n\n**Motivation and Scope**\n\nPrior work has examined robustness and generalization in sequential decision-making systems [5,6], but the specific fragility of memory-based self-improving agents\u2014particularly the impact of memory underspecification and curriculum effects\u2014remains poorly understood. Our study provides a focused, reproducible analysis of these issues, with the aim of informing future agent design and evaluation. We do not claim theoretical completeness, but rather seek to empirically characterize fragility and its causes in representative agent types.\n\nMethods\n\n**Agent Architectures and Selection Criteria**\n\nWe evaluate two agent types, selected based on prevalence in the literature, explicit use of structured memory, interpretability, and relevance to real-world sequential tasks:\n\n- **Method A: Retrieval-Augmented Language Model Agent**  \n  - *Architecture*: 12-layer transformer (768 hidden units, 12 attention heads; cf. [3]).  \n  - *Memory Bank*: Fixed-size list (max 500 entries) of textual episode summaries, each containing task description, actions, and outcomes.  \n  - *Embedding*: Each entry is encoded using mean-pooled transformer embeddings.  \n  - *Retrieval*: For each decision, compute cosine similarity between current context and all memory entries; return top-5 most similar.  \n  - *Redundancy Handling*: If multiple entries have cosine similarity > 0.98, retain only the most recent.  \n  - *Pruning*: FIFO policy when full.\n\n- **Method B: Memory-Augmented RL Agent**  \n  - *Architecture*: Deep Q-network (3-layer MLP, 256 units/layer, ReLU; cf. [4]).  \n  - *Memory Module*: Fixed-size list (max 500 entries) of tuples (state, action, reward, next state, textual summary).  \n  - *Embedding*: States are encoded via the penultimate MLP layer.  \n  - *Retrieval*: For each step, compute Euclidean distance between current state embedding and all memory entries; return top-3 nearest neighbors.  \n  - *Conflict Handling*: If identical state but different outcomes, both entries are retained and flagged (conflict flag in metadata).  \n  - *Pruning*: FIFO policy.\n\n**Memory Entry Generation and Validation**\n\n- *Extraction*: For each episode, the agent logs the initial task, stepwise actions, and final outcome. Key variables and constraints are extracted using regular expressions and environment metadata. For example, in the Tower of Hanoi, the extraction template is:  \n  ```\n  Task: Move N disks from peg A to peg B.\n  Actions: [(\"move disk 1 from A to C\"), ...]\n  Outcome: Success/Failure; Steps: X; Violations: [list]\n  ```\n  In navigation, the template is:  \n  ```\n  Task: Reach goal at (x, y).\n  Actions: [(\"up\", (i, j)), ...]\n  Outcome: Success/Failure; Steps: X; Obstacles hit: Y\n  ```\n- *Validation*: Each entry is checked for the presence of all required fields (task, actions, outcome). Entries missing any field (e.g., missing outcome or incomplete action sequence) are discarded. For the rubric-augmented condition, the extraction template requires explicit mention of all task-relevant variables and constraints, as determined by environment feedback (e.g., all pegs used, all obstacles encountered).\n- *Example of Incomplete Entry (discarded)*:  \n  ```\n  Task: Move 4 disks from A to B.\n  Actions: [(\"move disk 1 from A to C\")]\n  Outcome: [missing]\n  ```\n- *Example of Complete Entry*:  \n  ```\n  Task: Move 4 disks from A to B.\n  Actions: [(\"move disk 1 from A to C\"), (\"move disk 2 from A to B\"), ...]\n  Outcome: Success; Steps: 15; Violations: []\n  ```\n\n**Redundancy and Conflict Resolution**\n\n- *Near-Duplicate Detection*:  \n  - Method A: Cosine similarity > 0.98 \u2192 merge/discard as appropriate.  \n  - Method B: Euclidean distance < 0.05 \u2192 merge/discard.  \n- *Conflict Flagging*: For identical state-action pairs with different outcomes, both entries are retained with a conflict flag.\n\n**Manual Annotation Procedure and Qualitative Analysis**\n\n- *Sampling*: Randomly sample 50 memory entries per agent (stratified by task and run).\n- *Annotation*: Two annotators independently label each entry on three axes: specificity (high/medium/low), relevance (direct/partial/irrelevant), completeness (complete/partial/missing).\n- *Consensus*: Disagreements resolved by joint review.\n- *Agreement*: Cohen\u2019s \u03ba = 0.82 (\"almost perfect\" [7]).\n- *Examples*:  \n  - *High specificity, direct relevance, complete*:  \n    ```\n    Task: Reach goal at (7, 3). Actions: [(\"right\", (6,3)), (\"down\", (7,3))]. Outcome: Success; Steps: 2; Obstacles hit: 0\n    ```\n  - *Low specificity, partial relevance, partial*:  \n    ```\n    Task: Move disks. Actions: [(\"move\", ...)]. Outcome: [missing]\n    ```\n- *Integration*: Qualitative analysis revealed that incomplete or low-specificity entries were more frequent in runs with lower task completion rates, suggesting a link between memory underspecification and agent fragility.\n\n**Simulated Environments**\n\n- *Sequential Puzzle Solving*: Tower of Hanoi (3\u20135 disks), logic grid puzzles (3\u20135 variables). Observations: textual state descriptions; actions: discrete. Task complexity: optimal solution steps (3\u201315 for Hanoi, 5\u201312 for logic puzzles).\n- *Navigation Challenges*: 10x10 gridworlds with dynamic obstacles, stochastic movement, randomized goals. Observations: 2D arrays; actions: up/down/left/right. Task complexity: shortest path (8\u201320 steps), obstacles (1\u20135).\n- *Parameterization*: Task complexity, stochasticity (random action/environmental change per step, p=0.1 unless noted), reward structure (positive for completion, negative for failure/invalid actions).\n- *Benchmark*: 20 tasks per environment, generated with fixed random seeds. Task order randomized across runs to assess curriculum effects. Solvability verified automatically.\n\n**Agent Hyperparameters**\n\n- *Learning Rates*: 1e-4 (Method A), 5e-4 (Method B).\n-"}}